Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian gothic period drama; naturalistic faces, fog, candlelight, wool and silk textures; one consistent live-action look for all cast and spirits

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

Dense brown fog. Coal smoke. A black warehouse door bears the painted legend SCROOGE AND MARLEY. EBENEZER SCROOGE, elderly, sharp-nosed, thin blue lips, frosty white rime on brows and chin, red-rimmed eyes, black great-coat and muffler, edges through the crowd. People part from him. A blind man's dog tugs its owner into a doorway.

(SOUND: muffled wheezing coughs, boots stamping on cold stones, distant church bell)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A dismal office. Scrooge's small fire barely glows. Beyond a connecting door, BOB CRATCHIT, thin, threadbare, white comforter about his neck, copies letters at a desk beside one meagre coal. Fog seeps at the keyhole. Candles flare like ruddy smears.

(SOUND: scratch of quill, fog-muffled street noise, coal settling)

FRED, Scrooge's nephew, ruddy and handsome, eyes sparkling, breath still smoking from the cold, bursts in.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with Merry Christmas on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew, keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it.

FRED
I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Bob involuntarily applauds, then pokes out his last spark in fright.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle! And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves warmly, greeting Bob at the outer door. Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers, hats off.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Are there no prisons?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it. If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Mine occupies me constantly. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Darkness thickens. At closing, Scrooge buttons his great-coat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob snuffs his candle, winds his long white comforter, and hurries out.

(SOUND: door latch, Bob's quick steps fading on stone)

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS YARD - NIGHT

A lowering pile of building up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang about the black gateway. Scrooge gropes to his door, key in hand. The large knocker becomes MARLEY'S FACE: motionless wide eyes, spectacles on ghostly forehead, hair stirred as by hot air, livid colour.

(SOUND: distant gruff church bell, wind in the gateway)

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again. He turns the key sturdily, enters, lights his candle.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Wide dark stairs. Sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room checked: small fire, saucepan of gruel on the hob, dressing-gown on the wall. Scrooge double-locks the door, puts on dressing-gown, slippers, and nightcap, sits close to the low fire among Dutch Scripture tiles. Marley's face seems to swim on every tile.

(SOUND: fire's thin crackle, house settling)

SCROOGE
Humbug!

A disused bell begins to swing, softly, then loudly. Every bell in the house answers. They cease. A clanking rises from below, as of a heavy chain dragged over casks. The cellar-door booms. The noise climbs the stairs and comes straight toward the door.

(SOUND: cascade of bells; chain dragging on wood and stone; booming door)

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The dying flame leaps up. JACOB MARLEY'S GHOST passes through the heavy door: pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots, transparent body, chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, and steel purses clasped about his middle; folded kerchief bound about head and chin.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

The Ghost sits opposite the fireplace. Hair, skirts, and tassels stir as by hot vapour.

SCROOGE
You don't believe in me.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. It removes the bandage; the lower jaw drops upon its breast. Scrooge falls upon his knees.

(SOUND: appalling chain clank; jaw's smart click; Scrooge's choked breath)

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! Do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard. Is its pattern strange to you? Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself?

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. In life my spirit never roved beyond the narrow limits of our money-changing hole; and weary journeys lie before me!

MARLEY'S GHOST
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.

MARLEY'S GHOST
I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread.

SCROOGE
I—I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The Ghost binds the wrapper round its head. It walks backward; the window rises. Outside, phantoms drift in chains, moaning. One old ghost with an iron safe at its ankle cries piteously toward a woman and infant on a door-step. Marley joins the dirge and floats out. The night empties. Scrooge closes the window, tries the double-locked door, and falls asleep upon the bed without undressing.

(SOUND: mournful wailings on the wind; window sash; silence)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Deep dark. Church chimes strike twelve, though Scrooge went to bed past two. He touches his repeater: twelve. He dozes and wakes to the quarters.

(SOUND: deep dull hollow ONE)

Light flashes. Bed-curtains at his face are drawn aside by a hand. THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands close: like a child yet like an old man, white hair, unwrinkled face with tender bloom, long muscular bare arms and legs, pure white tunic, lustrous belt, branch of fresh green holly, dress trimmed with summer flowers; from its head a bright clear jet of light; a great extinguisher-cap under its arm. The figure fluctuates—now one arm, now twenty legs—then is itself again.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past. Rise! and walk with me!

Scrooge clasps his robe as they move toward the window.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit's hand touches Scrooge's heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Clear cold winter day, snow on the ground. Fields on either hand. A little market-town in the distance. Shaggy ponies and boys in carts shout Merry Christmas and part at cross-roads. Scrooge's lip trembles.

(SOUND: crisp air, merry distant shouts, pony hooves)

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us. The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

INT. BOARDING SCHOOL ROOM - DAY

Long bare melancholy room, deal forms and desks. BOY SCROOGE, small and alone, reads near a feeble fire. Adult Scrooge sits on a form and weeps. Through the window, storybook figures seem to pass—Ali Baba, a parrot, Friday.

(SOUND: mice behind panelling, drip from a water-spout, idle swing of a door)

SCROOGE
Poor boy! I wish—but it's too late now. There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something: that's all.

The Ghost waves its hand. The boy grows larger; the room darker. The door opens. LITTLE FAN, much younger, darts in and throws her arms about his neck.

LITTLE FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. And you're to be a man! And first, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

BOY SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature. But she had a large heart! She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

An old gentleman in a Welsh wig at a high desk—MR. FEZZIWIG—lays down his pen as the clock points to seven. YOUNG SCROOGE and DICK WILKINS hurry in.

(SOUND: clock chime, sharp clap of hands, shutters slammed and barred)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here!

The warehouse becomes a snug ball-room. A fiddler tunes. MRS. FEZZIWIG and three Miss Fezziwigs, employees, housemaid, baker, cook, milkman—all pour in. Dances whirl; Fezziwig cuts so deftly he seems to wink with his legs. At eleven, Mr. and Mrs. Fezziwig shake every hand at the door and wish Merry Christmas.

(MUSIC: lively fiddle — Sir Roger de Coverley)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOR - EVENING

YOUNG SCROOGE, now in prime of life, face beginning to harden with care and avarice, sits beside BELLE in mourning-dress; tears in her eyes in the Spirit's light.

(SOUND: low fire, distant street)

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me. A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty; and there is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth!

BELLE
You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Our contract is an old one. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

YOUNG SCROOGE
I was a boy.

BELLE
If you were free to-day, can even I believe that you would choose a dowerless girl—you who weigh everything by Gain? I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. Another shadow: Belle, now a comely matron, in a bright room full of noisy children; her husband home with Christmas toys. He mentions seeing Scrooge alone in his office, partner dying. Scrooge's sight grows dim.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

SCROOGE
No more! I don't wish to see it. Haunt me no longer!

Scrooge seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon the Spirit's head. Light still streams from under it. Exhausted, he reels to his own bed and sinks into heavy sleep.

(SOUND: struggle, heavy breathing, silence)

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

The bell strikes One. Ruddy light streams from the adjoining room. Scrooge shuffles to the door. A strange voice calls him by name.

(SOUND: deep ONE; roar of a mighty blaze)

The room is hung with living green—holly, mistletoe, ivy. A mighty blaze in the hearth. Heaped on the floor like a throne: turkeys, geese, joints, sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, oysters, chestnuts, apples, oranges, seething bowls of punch. Upon this couch sits THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT, a jolly Giant in a simple green robe bordered with white fur, bare breast and feet, holly wreath with shining icicles on dark brown free curls, glowing torch like Plenty's horn, empty rusted scabbard at his middle.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never. Spirit, conduct me where you will. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING - DAY

Snow on roofs; dirtier snow in furrows on the ground. People scrape pavements, fling snowballs, flock to church in best clothes. Poor folk carry dinners to bakers' shops. The Spirit sprinkles incense from his torch; angry jostlers grow good-humoured at once.

(SOUND: steeple bells, scrapers on stone, cheerful calls, snow plumping down)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given dinner. To a poor one most. Because it needs it most.

They pass on, invisible, to the suburbs.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT, poorly dressed but brave in ribbons, lays the cloth with BELINDA. MASTER PETER plunges a fork into potatoes, monstrous borrowed shirt-collar choking him. Two smaller Cratchits dance in, smelling goose. MARTHA arrives from work; they hide her as BOB CRATCHIT enters, threadbare clothes brushed, at least three feet of comforter hanging, TINY TIM upon his shoulder—little crutch, limbs in an iron frame.

(SOUND: crutch on floorboards; potato saucepan bubbling; children's shrieks of joy)

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

They fetch the goose. Grace. Mrs. Cratchit plunges the knife; stuffing gushes; Tiny Tim beats the table and cries Hurrah. The pudding arrives like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing in ignited brandy, holly on top. Later, chestnuts sputter; Bob serves hot mixture from a jug in two tumblers and a custard-cup without a handle.

(SOUND: hiss of gravy, crackle of chestnuts, soft family laughter)

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tim sits close; Bob holds his withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head. Bob raises a toast to Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast. Mrs. Cratchit bristles, then drinks for Bob's sake and the Day's. The family grows merry again; Tiny Tim sings in a plaintive little voice. Scrooge watches Tim until they fade in the Spirit's torchlight.

(MUSIC: Tiny Tim's thin plaintive carol)

EXT. BLEAK MOOR / LIGHTHOUSE / SHIP - NIGHT

Brief visions: miners' hut with generations singing; two lighthouse men wishing Merry Christmas over grog; ship's helmsman and watch humming a Christmas tune. Then a bright dry gleaming room—Fred's party. Fred laughs hugely; nieces and Topper join games of blind-man's buff and Yes and No. The answer is Uncle Scrooge. They drink his health anyway.

(SOUND: wind on moor; sea against rocks; hearty overlapping laughter)

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! He wouldn't take it from me, but may he have it, nevertheless. Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge tries to join the toast. The scene vanishes. The Ghost grows older; hair greys. Chimes ring three quarters past eleven.

INT. OPEN PLACE - NIGHT

From the Ghost's robe foldings cling two children: wretched, yellow, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish—a boy and girl.

(SOUND: midnight wind; distant bell beginning to gather)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost is gone. A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded in deep black, only one outstretched hand visible, comes like a mist along the ground.

(SOUND: twelve slow strokes; silence after the last)

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come? You are about to show me shadows of the things that have not happened, but will happen in the time before us. Is that so, Spirit?

The garment contracts as if the Spirit nods. Scrooge's legs tremble.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But I am prepared to bear you company, and do it with a thankful heart. Lead on!

EXT. THE EXCHANGE - DAY

Merchants chink money and converse. The Spirit points. Scrooge listens.

(SOUND: coins, watches snapped, dry laughter)

FAT MERCHANT
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MERCHANT HALES
When did he die?

FAT MERCHANT
Last night, I believe. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. Upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

RED-FACED GENTLEMAN
I don't mind going if a lunch is provided. But I must be fed, if I make one.

They stroll away. Two other wealthy men exchange only:

MR. CHISWICK
Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. LUTTRIDGE
So I am told. Cold, isn't it? Good morning!

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

Low-browed beetling shop of iron, rags, bottles, bones. OLD JOE, grey-haired, nearly seventy, smokes by a charcoal stove behind a curtain of tatters. A CHARWOMAN, MRS. DILBER the laundress, and an UNDERTAKER'S MAN enter with bundles and burst out laughing.

(SOUND: rusty door skreak; chalk on wall; bag of coins)

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! If we haven't all three met here without meaning it! Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did. Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose. Open that bundle, old Joe.

Joe appraises seal, pencil-case, sleeve-buttons; sheets, towels, teaspoons; then a heavy roll of bed-curtains, blankets, and a fine shirt taken off the corpse.

CHARWOMAN
This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DARK BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A bare uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet, a body, plundered, unwatched, unwept. Pale light falls on it. A cat tears at the door; rats gnaw beneath the hearth-stone. The Spirit points to the head. Scrooge cannot lift the cover.

(SOUND: cat at door; gnawing rats; Scrooge's shaking breath)

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. Let us go! If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. DEBTORS' ROOM - DAY

A mother, CAROLINE, paces with anxious eagerness. Her careworn young husband enters. Bad news—then:

(SOUND: children's hushed play; clock)

HUSBAND
He is past relenting. He is dead.

Relief breaks on both faces despite themselves—lighter hearts, brighter children. The only emotion shown is pleasure at the death.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and daughters sew. Peter reads. The noisy little Cratchits are still as statues.

(SOUND: soft fire; a single suppressed sob)

PETER
And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes. It makes them weak by candle-light; and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home, for the world.

Bob enters, comforter wound, cheerful for their sakes, then breaks.

BOB CRATCHIT
I went today. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child! My little child!

He goes upstairs, sits beside the child's place, kisses the little face, returns composed. He tells of Fred's kindness in the street. They vow never to forget Tiny Tim.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY

Walled in by houses, overrun by grass and weeds, choked with too much burying. The Spirit stands among the graves and points down to one. Scrooge advances trembling.

(SOUND: wind in weeds; far iron gate)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Spirit points downward still.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

Scrooge creeps forward and reads upon the neglected stone: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

The finger points from the grave to him and back again.

SCROOGE
Spirit! hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope!

The hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE
Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life! I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He clutches the spectral hand. The Phantom repulses him. The hood and dress shrink, collapse, and dwindle into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is his own. The bed is his own. Sunlight. Scrooge scrambles out, sobbing and laughing, face wet with tears, wrestling with stockings.

(SOUND: lustiest church peals—clash, clang, hammer, ding, dong, bell)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees!

SCROOGE
They are not torn down—they are not torn down, rings and all. I am here—the shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled. I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He runs to the window, opens it. No fog. Clear, bright, jovial cold. Golden sunlight. A boy in Sunday clothes below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner? Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there—not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck! Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot. Scrooge rubs his hands.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

He pats the knocker as he waits.

SCROOGE
I shall love it, as long as I live! What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!—Here's the Turkey! Hallo! Whoop! How are you! Merry Christmas!

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Scrooge, dressed in his best, walks with hands behind him, smiling at everyone. Good-humoured fellows call Merry Christmas; he answers. He overtakes the Portly Gentleman.

(SOUND: bright street bustle, church bells far off, footsteps)

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. A merry Christmas to you, sir! Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

He whispers in the gentleman's ear.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you come and see me?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. Bless you!

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - DAY

Scrooge sidles his face round the door. The table is spread in great array. Fred and his wife look up.

(SOUND: warm room murmur, fire)

SCROOGE
Fred! It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred nearly shakes his arm off. Hearty welcome; wonderful party, wonderful games, wonderful happiness.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

Next morning. Scrooge arrives early, door wide open to the Tank. The clock strikes nine. A quarter past. Bob, full eighteen minutes and a half late, sheds hat and comforter and drives his pen as if to overtake the hour.

(SOUND: clock strike; frantic quill)

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He leaps from his stool and digs Bob in the waistcoat; Bob staggers back into the Tank, eyeing the ruler.

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Snow bright. Scrooge walks with Tiny Tim on his shoulder—Tim alive, laughing, little crutch swinging. Bob and Mrs. Cratchit beside them. Fred waves from across the way. Church bells pour over the city.

(SOUND: joyful peal of bells; Tim's bright laugh; footsteps in snow)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew. May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed—

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.

THE END
